WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.810
Film Courage: Writing style versus voice?

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.199
What are the differences?

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:07.509
Jennifer Brody: Well I always talk about what
is the difference between character and voice.

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:11.410
So character is someone in a story and they
are going to have a voice which is going to

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:16.960
be the way they talk but then the over-arching
voice of the story is more about the way it

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.089
is told like the style and voice in terms
of the writing an sometimes that is also the

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:25.740
characters voice, especially if you’re writing
in first-person or a third-person clothes,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.689
because you are trying to encapsulate what
they see and feel….what was the other thing

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:32.689
you asked about?

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:33.689
Film Courage: Oh…yes…writing style?

00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:34.689
Jennifer Brody: Style!

00:00:34.689 --> 00:00:35.850
Yes, they kind of overlap a little bit.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:41.580
I mean the style of writing and the voice…voice
is really trying to capture the feeling of

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.610
the narrator’s voice or the character’s
voice or the way that the story is told.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:50.020
The style of it (I guess) would be more like
the way that you’re writing the book.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.900
But there’s a lot of overlap between all
three of those areas.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.190
When you pick up a book that has really strong
voice, you are usually hearing the character

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.740
and hearing the world.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.979
And every book exists within its on world
even books that aren’t specifically sci-fi

00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:05.979
fantasy.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:10.820
You’re still crafting a world even if it’s
something like a thriller, it has a feel and

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.860
an internal logic to the world and it’s
not necessarily the real world, even.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.380
if it’s a contemporary story.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.350
Something like GONE GIRL exists in its own
real world.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.420
I don’t think those people exist in the
real world.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.210
Those characters to me are very unrealistic
in a lot of respects, but in the world of

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.719
that story they are all like that.

00:01:28.719 --> 00:01:32.960
And so I don’t think that is a real world
thing, even if it takes place in the here

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:33.960
and now.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.409
But there is a specific way that she (Author
Gillian Flynn) writes her stories and that

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:41.420
she transports you into them.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.909
Film Courage: Hooking in the reader from the
beginning?

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:51.219
I’m sure it’s going to vary especially
within the YA world and the different genres

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:54.670
that there are whether it’s fiction, steampunk,
whatever it may be.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.039
Jennifer Brody: Oh yeah!

00:01:56.039 --> 00:01:57.079
Steampunk, what fun!

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:01.179
I think hooking the reading is just so important
regardless of what you’re writing.

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:06.159
I just think for me, if I’m not really feeling
a book (and I don’t put a lot of books down)

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:10.160
but 50-100 pages is a long time and if I’m
not feeling it and you’d be surprised how

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.740
many books don’t really work on that level,
I may not finish it.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:19.840
And especially if you’re dealing with more
commercial genres it becomes even more important,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.629
younger readers, I think it becomes even more
important.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:29.599
That said I don’t think you need to rush
or sacrifice the storytelling to make it so

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:31.120
hooky.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.989
In a certain respect I personally wouldn't
want to do that but I think getting into the

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:39.900
story right away, having something exciting
happen in the beginning.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:46.360
In my book I chose to write a prologue that
showed kind of what happened before when the

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.280
surface of the earth was destroyed so I told
it from (if you can imagine) the president’s

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.620
daughters point of view as they’re evacuating
the White House.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:01.100
So young girl being evacuated and I did that
for a couple of reasons.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.799
One was that I wanted to have something really
exciting and really relatable that happened

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:08.580
right off the bat because I think that this
will hook you in and make you want to read

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:09.580
more.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.150
But there are other ways to hook in somebody,
like having a great character.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:17.920
Having central mystery is something I teach
about a lot which is there is obviously a

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:24.140
mystery genre where the whole story is crafted
but that said you can have a story that has

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.280
smaller mysteries that are revealed in the
beginning of a book.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.690
A good examples is in THE HUNGER GAMES.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:34.120
They talk about the reaping which is the big
thing that happens right away in THE HUNGER

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.549
GAMES but they say the word “The Reaping”
7 or 8 times before they finally tell you

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:42.780
what it is and you find out fairly early in
the story but still you’re kind of reading

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:43.780
like “What is this?

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:44.780
What is this thing that is happening?

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:45.780
What is this big event?”

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.730
So I think that’s another way to kind of
get somebody interested and trying to figure

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.290
out what’s going on.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.609
Film Courage: Too much conflict?

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:55.609
Too little conflict?

00:03:55.609 --> 00:03:59.980
Do YA readers want more conflict than maybe
adult readers?

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:05.100
Jennifer Brody: I think you have more leeway
to do some bigger kind of more epic sweeping

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.609
sort of things like what you see in a STAR
WARS movie, for example.

00:04:09.609 --> 00:04:13.420
You know too little conflict in any story
or too little tension and the story is going

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.600
to be really boring and nobody is going to
be interested.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.301
I think you need to have conflict and tension
almost if not in every scene of your book

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:23.340
but pretty much in every scene, even scenes
that are character driven.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:28.900
I always think you want to look for how can
you ramp up the tension in that, even if it’s

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.479
just a dialogue exchange you try to have moments
that are ratcheting up the tension.

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:37.360
So a good example of that is something like
in the beginning of THE SHINING (the movie).

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.880
I also love the book, but the film there’s
a scene where it’s the mom talking to Child

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:42.880
Protective Services.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.720
So you’re learning information about the
fact that the father may have been abusive

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:47.720
toward the son.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:51.620
But it’s just a dialogue seen but the the
whole time she is smoking a cigarette and

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.259
the ash just gets longer and longer and you’re
like “Oh my God!

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:56.259
Ash your cigarette!”

00:04:56.259 --> 00:04:57.260
You get so tense for her.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.410
You’re like “It’s going to fall all
over you!”

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.940
And she doesn’t and it’s somehow this
genius way of just keeping tension.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.289
So there’s always little things you can
look at in a scene to kind of keep the tension

00:05:07.289 --> 00:05:10.889
going and then obviously there are big conflict
scenes where there is a fight or actually

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:16.490
something major happens or a major obstacle
but I think throughout a story, that’s what

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.789
keeps you reading and people would be like
“Oh, it’s getting worse.

00:05:18.789 --> 00:05:21.400
It’s getting so much worse for these characters.”

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.910
Or even like a book series like Game of Thrones,
you’re like “Oh, my God!

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.770
Things are still happening and happening.”

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.290
But it’s like, that is what keeps the story
going.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.830
A story about conflict is a really boring
story.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.819
I think Kurt Vonnegut famously said make great
characters and then make bad things happen

00:05:36.819 --> 00:05:37.819
to them.

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:41.840
Film Courage: If a book is not part of a series,
let’s suppose it’s just going to be a

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.610
standalone book, is the protagonist always
victorious in the end?

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:51.110
Jennifer Brody: No and you see that a lot
(my book is considered dystopian), especially

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:58.069
in dystopian series where a lot of times they’re
not victorious and I when I teach structure

00:05:58.069 --> 00:06:02.930
I talk about that where there is going to
be a moment at the end of the story where

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.259
a character makes a decision.

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:08.830
They need to make a choice that really reveals
their moral caliber, but that doesn’t mean

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.819
that the choice they make has to be the right
one.

00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:14.740
A good example is Romeo and Juliet, right?

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:20.319
Romeo does make a choice at the end and it’s
a terrible choice and it ends tragically for

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:21.319
him.

00:06:21.319 --> 00:06:22.500
He made the wrong decision.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.810
So no…it doesn’t always have to result
in a happy ending and I think some of the

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.819
greatest stories sometimes don’t.

00:06:30.819 --> 00:06:35.260
That said, I think if you are going to go
for a really kind of…I won’t call it a

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:41.860
negative ending but an ending where the protagonist
is not victorious it makes your job so much

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:42.860
harder in a lot of ways.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.310
I think you really need to pull that off because
your readers are probably going to be a little

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.970
bit mad at you to a certain extent.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:55.750
So I think it needs to have a compelling reason
why to have that sort of ending or a compelling

00:06:55.750 --> 00:07:01.220
reason why you wanted to do that for your
story because we’ve all been there where

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.199
we’ve seen something with an ending where
we go “Wow!

00:07:04.199 --> 00:07:05.610
That was really dark.”

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:10.919
And sometimes I love it and even in films
where classically a lot that is focus-group

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:17.680
tested out of the film, but films like I AM
LEGEND or the third TERMINATOR film where

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.210
something really dark happens at the end but
then it’s awesome.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.900
And of course if you get into more like horror
movies and things like that, then it’s almost

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:29.540
expected because a lot of times in those,
the antagonists becomes the protagonist.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.840
So when we’re watching NIGHTMARE ON ELM
STREET, right..okay..maybe originally we were

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:33.930
rooting for Johnny Depp and the teens.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:37.250
But at a certain point Freddy is who we are
rooting for.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:43.470
At a certain point you’re showing up at
these films to see Michael Myers or Leatherface

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.550
or Pinhead (I’m a huge HELLRAISER fan).

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:51.110
But you’re showing up for them more so almost
then who are the classic protagonist and you

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:55.050
kind of want to see them win in the end and
boy would we be upset if Freddy was gone at

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.050
the end.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:57.580
I know Freddy’s done at the end of the movie
but he’s not really gone.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:58.580
Question for the Viewers: What’s your favorite
author?

00:07:58.580 --> 00:07:58.585
Why do you love them?

